  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
    "lesson_core_move": "Evaluate high-frequency limits of |$H(j\\omega)$| and $\\angle$ $H(j\\omega)$ to predict how an LTI system passes, attenuates, or phase-shifts rapidly varying sinusoidal components.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to High-Frequency Behavior",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $\\omega$ is large, the question is not the full shape of $H(j\\omega)$ but what $|H(j\\omega)|$ and $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ approach. Those two limits are the high-frequency signature of the system, because they tell you whether a rapid sinusoidal component is passed, attenuated, or shifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|,\\qquad \\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\angle H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a sinusoidal component $A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)$, the output keeps the same frequency; only the amplitude and phase change. So once the high-frequency gain and phase are known, you can read the fate of the rapid component directly from $|H(j\\omega)|$ and $\\angle H(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=A|H(j\\omega_0)|\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi+\\angle H(j\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A simple first-order response such as $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$ shows the base case: the dominant power of $j\\omega$ controls the limit, the magnitude shrinks toward $0$, and the phase settles to a constant lag. That is the pattern you will reuse in the later high-frequency examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating High-Frequency Magnitude Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|$ for $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{3j\\omega+2}{2j\\omega-5}$ and decide whether very high-frequency components are attenuated, passed, or amplified.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a high-frequency magnitude question, so the cue is the limit as $\\omega\\to\\infty$ of $|H(j\\omega)|$. For a rational transfer function, the lower-order terms disappear at high frequency, so the leading powers of $j\\omega$ determine the gain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{3j\\omega+2}{2j\\omega-5}\\sim \\frac{3j\\omega}{2j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now take the magnitude of the dominant coefficient ratio. Because the numerator and denominator have the same highest power of $j\\omega$, the asymptotic magnitude is just the ratio of the leading coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{3j\\omega+2}{2j\\omega-5}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{3}{2}\\right|=\\frac{3}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $\\frac{3}{2}>1$, the system amplifies very high-frequency sinusoidal components. This example stays on magnitude only; the phase limit is the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating High-Frequency Phase Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\angle H(j\\omega)$ for $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1-j\\omega}{1+j\\omega}$ and interpret the asymptotic phase shift on a rapid sinusoid.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the phase version of the high-frequency test, so the cue is $\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\angle H(j\\omega)$, not the magnitude. For a rational transfer function, the highest-power terms control the limiting angle because lower-order terms fade away.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1-j\\omega}{1+j\\omega}\\sim \\frac{-j\\omega}{j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the asymptotic angle of each leading factor: the numerator approaches $-\\pi/2$ and the denominator approaches $\\pi/2$. Subtracting the denominator phase from the numerator phase gives the constant high-frequency phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\angle\\frac{1-j\\omega}{1+j\\omega}=-\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}=-\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the high-frequency phase limit is $-\\pi$, which is a sign inversion of the rapid sinusoid. This example stays on phase only; the magnitude story was the previous step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using Dominant Powers of $j\\omega$ in High-Frequency Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Use dominant powers of $j\\omega$ to evaluate the high-frequency magnitude limit of $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{3(j\\omega)^2-2j\\omega+1}{(j\\omega)^3+4(j\\omega)^2-5}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a high-frequency magnitude question with extra lower-order clutter, so the cue is the degree mismatch in the rational transfer function. When $\\omega$ is large, keep the highest powers of $j\\omega$ and ignore the terms that vanish after factoring.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{(j\\omega)^2\\left(3-\\frac{2}{j\\omega}+\\frac{1}{(j\\omega)^2}\\right)}{(j\\omega)^3\\left(1+\\frac{4}{j\\omega}-\\frac{5}{(j\\omega)^3}\\right)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cancel the common $(j\\omega)^2$ factor. One power of $j\\omega$ remains in the denominator, so the magnitude must decay toward zero no matter what the lower-order terms do.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\left|H(j\\omega)\\right|=\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{1}{j\\omega}\\right|=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the high-frequency magnitude limit is $0$, which means the system attenuates rapidly varying sinusoidal components. The dominant-power shortcut is the point: the degree gap decides the limit before any full expansion is needed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "High-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Predicting Rapid-Sinusoid Output from High-Frequency Gain and Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For very large $\\omega_0$, predict the output of $x(t)=3\\cos(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/6)$ when the high-frequency limit of $H(j\\omega)$ is $-2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the output-prediction version of the high-frequency test, so the cue is a rapid sinusoid together with the asymptotic gain and phase of $H(j\\omega)$. At very large $\\omega_0$, the output keeps the same frequency and only the amplitude and phase change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)\\mapsto A|H(j\\omega_0)|\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi+\\angle H(j\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $H(j\\omega)\\to -2$, so the high-frequency gain is $2$ and the phase shift is $\\pi$. That means the rapid sinusoid is amplified by a factor of $2$ and shifted by a half cycle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3\\cos(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/6)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y(t)\\approx 3\\cdot 2\\cos(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/6+\\pi)=6\\cos(\\omega_0 t+5\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $6\\cos(\\omega_0 t+5\\pi/6)$, which is the same-frequency sinusoid with doubled amplitude and a $\\pi$ phase shift. This completes the transfer from asymptotic gain and phase to a concrete rapid-sinusoid output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
